fe11303a8d8a3161093eed68065427ebe86bf1a4e84ff20d31e4d21","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Politics & Government","secondarySubjects":["Nature & Environment","Society & Culture"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily discusses the political struggle against fracking in Texas, intersecting with environmental and societal issues, which points to Politics & Government as the dominant subject.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"0c37362bfcc445d502b816228e400893f12e9e92d274615b1b44d09b32c00d09","summary":"When philosophy professor Adam Briggle moved to Denton, Texas, he had never heard of fracking. Only five years later he would successfully lead a citizens' initiative to ban hydraulic fracturing in Denton -- the first Texas town to challenge the oil and gas industry. On his journey to learn about fracking and its effects, he leaped from the ivory tower into the fray. Briggle brings us to town hall debates and neighborhood meetings where citizens wrestle with issues few fully understand. Is fracking safe? How does it affect the local economy? Why are bakeries prohibited in neighborhoods while gas wells are permitted next to playgrounds? In his quest for answers Briggle meets people like Cathy McMullen. Her neighbors' cows asphyxiated after drinking fracking fluids, and her orchard was razed to make way for a pipeline. Cathy did not consent to drilling, but those who profited lived far out of harm's way. Briggle's first instinct was to think about fracking -- deeply. Drawing on philosophers from Socrates to Kant, but also on conversations with engineers, legislators, and industry representatives, he develops a simple theory to evaluate fracking: we should give those at risk to harm a stake in the decisions we make, and we should monitor for and correct any problems that arise.","publicationYear":2015,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Oil wells","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-field-philosopher-s-guide-to-fracking-how-one-texas-town-stood-up-to-big-oil-and-gas-adam-brig"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Gas wells","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-field-philosopher-s-guide-to-fracking-how-one-texas-town-stood-up-to-big-oil-and-gas-adam-brig"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Political aspects","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-field-philosopher-s-guide-to-fracking-how-one-texas-town-stood-up-to-big-oil-and-gas-adam-brig"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Petroleum industry and trade","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-field-philosopher-s-guide-to-fracking-how-one-texas-town-stood-up-to-big-oil-and-gas-adam-brig"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Referendum","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-field-philosopher-s-guide-to-fracking-how-one-texas-town-stood-up-to-big-oil-and-gas-adam-brig"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Hydraulic fracturing","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-field-philosopher-s-guide-to-fracking-how-one-texas-town-stood-up-to-big-oil-and-gas-adam-brig"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Texas, politics and government","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-field-philosopher-s-guide-to-fracking-how-one-texas-town-stood-up-to-big-oil-and-gas-adam-brig"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Environmental responsibility","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-field-philosopher-s-guide-to-fracking-how-one-texas-town-stood-up-to-big-oil-and-gas-adam-brig"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"New York Times reviewed","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-field-philosopher-s-guide-to-fracking-how-one-texas-town-stood-up-to-big-oil-and-gas-adam-brig"}],"displaySummary":"When philosophy professor Adam Briggle moved to Denton, Texas, he had never heard of fracking. Only five years later he would successfully lead a citizens' initiative to ban hydraulic fracturing in Denton -- the first Texas town to challenge the oil and gas industry. On his journey to learn about fracking and its effects, he leaped from the ivory tower into the fray. Briggle brings us to town hall debates and neighborhood meetings where citizens wrestle with issues few fully understand. Is fracking safe? How does it affect the local economy? Why are bakeries prohibited in neighborhoods while gas wells are permitted next to playgrounds? In his quest for answers Briggle meets people like Cathy McMullen. Her neighbors' cows asphyxiated after drinking fracking fluids, and her orchard was razed to make way for a pipeline. Cathy did not consent to drilling, but those who profited lived far out of harm's way. Briggle's first instinct was to think about fracking -- deeply. Drawing on philosophers from Socrates to Kant, but also on conversations with engineers, legislators, and industry representatives, he develops a simple theory to evaluate fracking: we should give those at risk to harm a stake in the decisions we make, and we should monitor for and correct any problems that arise.","primaryTopic":"Business, Capitalism & Corporations","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Politics & Government","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Politics & Government","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Politics & Government","mappedSubject":"Politics & Government","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book primarily discusses the political struggle against fracking in Texas, intersecting with environmental and societal issues, which points to Politics & Government as the dominant subject.","id":"subject-evidence-book-a-field-philosopher-s-guide-to-fracking-how-one-texas-town-stood-up-to-big-oil-and-gas-adam